[%think 31389] [%out 31750] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1615]
**Situation:** I'm a knight up. Black's queen on b7 threatens ...Qxe4+, winning
the undefended e4 knight with check. **Constraint:** Black's queen and rook are
lined up on the b-file against b2. If my queen stops guarding b2, then ...Qxb2
is checkmate. - That rules out Qe3, Qd3 and Qe1. Each would lose to ...Qxb2#. -
Moving the knight (Nd6, Nxc5) opens e4 for ...Qe4+. The queen then gets active,
and Black can win back the e5, g2 and h2 pawns. **Qc2:** - It still guards b2,
so the b-file pressure does nothing. - It protects the e4 knight along the
c2–e4 diagonal and keeps c4 covered. - It lines up the queen behind the knight
against h7. Next I threaten Ng5 followed by Qxh7#. - If Black plays ...Qb3 to
offer a queen trade, I take it. Being a piece up in the endgame is a clear win.
} 23... Rfd8 24. Nxc5 { [%emt 0:03:42] [%think 21227] [%out 21686] [%in 2]
[%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1641] **思路：** 黑方 c5 兵没有任何子保护：黑后在
b7 碰不到 c5，两个车也都不在相关线上。白马 e4→c5 可以白吃一兵，同时攻击黑后
b7，并威胁 a6 和 e6。 **安全性检查：** - 马离开 e4 后，黑后的斜线打开到 g2。但
g2 有白后 c2 沿第二横线保护，所以 ...Qxg2 会直接丢后。 - 黑方可以先走
...Rxd1+。白方用 h1 车回吃 Rxd1，让白后留在 c2 继续保护 b2 和 g2。
黑后仍然被马攻击，必须走开。 - 黑后如果走 ...Qb6（同时打 c5 马和 b2），白方有
Nd7，马在 d7 同时捉后和 b8 车，而且有 d1 车保护。 - 黑后如果走 ...Qc7（同时打
c5 马和 e5 兵），白方可以走 Nxa6 同时捉后和车，或者稳妥地走 Nd3。 - ...Qxb2+ 在
Qxb2、Rxb2+、Kxb2 之后，黑方净亏一车。 所以吃 c5 兵安全，白方将多一马一兵。 }
24... Qb6 25. Nd7 { [%emt 0:02:22] [%think 13607] [%out 14025] [%in 2]
[%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1662] Black's queen on b6 attacks my knight on c5,
so the knight has to move. The best square is d7. **Nd7 forks the queen on b6
and the rook on b8 at once.** The d8 rook can take the knight, but my d1 rook
recaptures. Black's main replies: - **…Rxd7 Rxd7:** Black gives up a rook for
my knight. I'm then up a full rook plus a pawn. - **…Rxd1+:** I must recapture
with the h1 rook. If my queen recaptures instead, it leaves b2. Then …Qxb2 is
mate, because the rook on b8 covers b2 and my king can't take. After the rook
recaptures, the queen and b8 rook are still forked, and next move I take on b8.
- **…Qxb2+:** Qxb2 Rxb2+ Kxb2, and Black has lost even more material. - **…Qb3
(offering the queen trade):** I play Qxb3 Rxb3, then Nf6+. After …gxf6 Rxd8+
I'm up a rook. After …Kh8, Rxd8 is mate. Taking with Nxb8 first also wins.
Either way I win at least a rook for the knight, and the game is decisively
winning. } 25... Rxd7 26. Rxd7 { [%emt 0:00:40] [%think 3477] [%out 3702]
[%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1686] The d-file is clear from d2 to d6, so
26. Rxd7 takes back the rook on d7. After the trade White is up a rook and a
pawn. Black has no dangerous follow-up: - **...Qxb2+:** Qxb2 Rxb2+ Kxb2, and
Black has lost too much material. - **...Qg1+:** Rh1xg1 takes the queen. The
rook on d7 also attacks f7. Together with the queen on the c2–h7 diagonal, this
keeps pressure on Black's king. Other moves are worse. Qxh7+ gives away the
queen, and then ...Qxb2 is mate. } 26... g6 27. Rhd1 { [%emt 0:03:24]
